  {
    "id": "EQ2-02",
    "question": "An employee wants to work remotely from outside their country while handling personal data. What approval and data-transfer safeguards apply?",
    "top_score": 0.5309131326,
    "top_vector_score": 0.544161618,
    "top_keyword_score": 0.5,
    "top_section": "REMOTE WORK POLICY",
    "retrieval_seconds": 0.4860363689949736,
    "answer_seconds": 6.473156081992784,
    "total_seconds": 6.959223876998294,
    "answer": "- **Remote-work approval:** Remote days must be agreed with the direct manager in advance. Working outside the country of employment also requires **prior written approval from HR and Legal**. If the arrangement is fully remote (5 days/week), **VP-level approval** is additionally required. *(Remote Work Policy)*\n\n- **Cross-border data approval:** Any cross-border transfer of personal data must be approved by the **DPO** at **dpo@acme.com**. If data is transferred outside the EEA, it must use an approved mechanism: **Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs), an adequacy decision, or Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs)**. *(Data Privacy and GDPR Compliance)*\n\n- **Security safeguards:** Data in transit must use **TLS 1.2 or higher**, data at rest must use **AES-256**, and the laptop must have full-disk encryption enabled. Sensitive data may not be stored on personal devices, personal email may not be used for business communications, and security controls may not be bypassed. *(Information Security Policy; Acceptable Use Policy)*",
