  {
    "id": "EQ2-04",
    "question": "A developer receives HTTP 429 while using an OAuth access token. What does the error mean, how should they retry, and how long is the token valid?",
    "top_score": 0.41900798689999996,
    "top_vector_score": 0.464654267,
    "top_keyword_score": 0.3125,
    "top_section": "ERROR CODES REFERENCE",
    "retrieval_seconds": 0.7342248700006166,
    "answer_seconds": 2.043167381998501,
    "total_seconds": 2.777418415003922,
    "answer": "HTTP 429 means the API rate limit has been exceeded.\n\n- Do not retry immediately.\n- Wait for the number of seconds specified in the `Retry-After` header. The `X-RateLimit-Reset` header also shows the Unix timestamp when the limit resets.\n- Implement exponential backoff with jitter for retries.\n\nThe OAuth access token is valid for 3,600 seconds (1 hour). Use the refresh token to obtain a new access token before it expires. The refresh token itself is valid for 30 days.\n\n(Sections: **ERROR CODES REFERENCE**, **RATE LIMITING**, **API AUTHENTICATION**)",
