    "answer_seconds": 5.329594356997404,
    "total_seconds": 5.6289035600057105,
    "answer": "There is a policy conflict, so ACME\u2019s rules do not clearly determine whether the employee is eligible for remote work at this point.\n\n- The **Onboarding Process** says Day 90 is when the probationary period ends and remote work eligibility begins.\n- The **Remote Work Policy** says employees may work remotely only after completing their **6-month probationary period**.\n\nBecause the employee has completed Day 90 but not 6 months, the context does not say which rule controls.\n\nIf remote work is treated as allowed, the Remote Work Policy rules would apply:\n\n- Up to **3 days per week** remotely.\n- Remote days must be agreed with the direct manager in advance.\n- The employee must maintain a safe, ergonomic, distraction-free workspace.\n- Core hours of **10:00 AM\u20133:00 PM local time** must be maintained.\n- The employee must be reachable via Slack and respond within **30 minutes during core hours**.\n- Fully remote work requires VP-level approval and is reviewed annually.\n- Working remotely outside the country of employment requires prior written approval from HR and Legal.",
